o these  genes are involved in antibiotic  resistance to several different  compounds we also identified a class a  beta-lactamase shown here so these are  just the three most abundant but we  actually found more than this throughout  the data and then what you can see over  here these each of these plots is  showing the relative abundance of that  gene in the community plotted against  the mean chemical concentration of  triclosan in that in that community and  so you do see a statistically  significant correlation between higher  levels of triclosan and higher relative  abundance of these antibiotic resistance  genes in some samples but not all  samples and so this is is the first  instance where we've been able to  correlate the composition of the  microbiome and specifically looking at  antibiotic resistance genes and  connecting that to concentrations of  triclosan and dust and so erica is  currently working on a larger follow-up  study to this to look at other types of  buildings and environments to see if  this if this pattern exists you know  there as well but I don't want to alarm  you too much so we also as part of this  study compared the overall abundance of  antibiotic resistance genes to other  environments that have been studied and  so just to orient you with this diagram  this is these are violin plots which are  basically box Fox here you have the  median and the first and third quartiles  indicated in the boxes here and so these  lines on the outside simply represent  the distribution of data points along  that distribution and so this is from  our study these are the total basically  the total abundance of resistance genes  that we identified and then this is a  compilation of several other studies  looking at letak gyms and homes and then  finally over here this is looking at  antibiotic resistance genes in a cohort  of human microbiome samples and so we  can see that even though we have some  elevated resistance genes in our sample  and also in other built environments  it's also still high in the human  microbiome and so we don't fully  understand why this is the case yet but  this is an important factor to consider  when we're thinking about how we apply  both antimicrobials in the environment  and also antibiotics as humans you know  taking antibiotic medications these  things can potentially have impacts on  selecting for antibiotic resistance in  the micro biomes that live both in the  environment and also in our bodies and  so there's a lot of additional work to  be done in this area and this is kind of  an emerging area of research but we  think it's really important to consider  and so many of you might have heard  about this news last week and we think  this is good news the FDA recently  recommended that 19 of these  antimicrobial chemicals that are  commonly used in hand soaps and personal  care products these are no longer going  to be allowed in the US and so this does  include triclosan you can visit this  link here for more information but  basically they concluded that there  wasn't enough scientific evidence to  support the claim that add addition of  these chemicals was any more effective  than just using soap and water so we  were really happy to see this and with  you know timely publication of our these  results were actually just published  today from from the study I just showed  you so I encourage you to go look this  up in environmental science and  technology yeah so that's kind of a  summary of the research that we've done  at the bio bees Center over the last six  years oh sorry I have one more study  almost forgot about one of my favorites  here so now at this point I've shown you  how architectural features including  ventilation and space type can influence  the microbiome I've shown you how  interactions with surfaces and  application of antimicrobial chemicals  could also potentially have an impact  and so one of the observations that was  continually made through all of these  studies is that many of the bacteria  that we usually observe in the micro in  the indoor microbiome are bacteria that  are commonly associated with humans and  so we presume that the microbes are  getting there through humans shedding  them into the indoor environment but no  one had actually measured this before  and so this last study i'm going to show  you focuses on the human microbial cloud  and what I mean by that is the microbial  cloud you can think of as all of the  particles that are being shed by a  person into their surroundings and those  particles have microbes attached to them  and so this work was also led by dr.  James meadow and he was asking the  question how do individuals micro biomes  actually colonize the built environment  and so in order to do this you need a  fairly well controlled system or  container where you can sample a human  microbial cloud and what you see here in  this background picture this is the  climate chamber at the energy studies in  buildings laboratory in Portland Oregon  and so you can see two people sitting in  chairs here and I'll show you just in a  slide the experimental setup in a little  bit more detail but what you see here  there's air samplers set up around each  person so at both head height and lower  closer to the four floor and so we  sampled the air microbiome around people  inside of this closed room so here's a  schematic diagram of what this climate  chamber looks like it's about the size  of a you know a small office here's a  person for scale and so inside this  chamber all of the walls have radiant  heating panels and so you can adjust  both the temperature and humidity of the  room there's also this air supply duct  where you can control whether what type  of ventilation you have whether its  natural ventilation or indoor mechanical  ventilation and we also have a ceiling  fan up here so we can really control all  of the building variables that we think  matter for contributing to microbial  ecology and I should mention that this  climate chamber was originally developed  for architectural research on thermal  comfort research and so we've kind of  co-opted it for our own purposes and  using to use it as a giant petri dish of  sorts and so on the right side here you  can this is illustrating the  experimental setup of having a person  sitting in the chamber and so what they  did was they put up a plastic sheet just  across the width of the room here  effectively creating two smaller rooms  and so half of the room remained  unoccupied you see an empty chair here  the top down and we set up air samplers  just around the outside of that chair to  collect air on the other side of the of  the wall here we had a person sitting in  a chair for two or four hours and again  collecting air samples and so we use the  same cultivation independent techniques  of extracting DNA from these air filters  and amplifying bacterial genes and then  comparing the community composition in  those different space types and what we  found was that an occupied space does in  fact have a distinct microbiome from  that adjacent unoccupied space and so  again I'm showing some ordination  diagrams like I showed you earlier where  each of these points represents a single  air sample that was taken from around  the perimeter of one of those chairs so  the triangles represent the unoccupied  room and the circles represent the  occupied room from three different  subjects there were ten subjects total  in this study and so you can see by  looking at each of these that the  occupied spaces are quite distinct from  the unoccupied spaces and on the right  side of the diagram here this is just  clustering all of those data points  together in a dendrogram and so you can  see that most of the human associated  rooms tend to cluster down here whereas  the unoccupied spaces cluster up here  and have a lot more variability and so  this was the first evidence to really  demonstrate that an empty room looks  different from a room with a person  sitting in it even just for a couple of  hours and we consuming further than that  and look at some of the distinguishing  bacterial taxa that were identified in  though in those air samples and so here  we're showing again another dendogram  clustering several samples from five  different individuals and then along the  bottom here we have some of the most  distinguishing bacteria taxa that we  could find to that characterize each of  those people and so you can see that  this person a here in Orange has a  pretty distinct signature of  lactobacillus Chris bodice whereas most  of the other people here do not  similarly subjects d here and I should  mention that both subjects a and D were  female and so both of these organisms  lactobacillus and gardnerella are  organisms that you commonly find in the  female urogenital tract and so we're  also detecting those in the air sampled  around them  and so you can see that this individual  had strong signature of this organism  that was not as common in the others and  so on and so forth you can see that for  each of these people you have kind of  these unique fingerprints or microbial  signatures and again this is just from  sampled from the air around the people  this isn't from their skin microbiome or  you know sampling the person directly  themselves and so this shows us that  just by sampling error we can identify  if a person is in a room if you're  interested in hearing a little bit more  about this science friday did a piece on  this back in january and here i am  sitting as a guinea pig in the climate  chamber so i encourage you to go check  that out if you're interested but we  don't have time to watch that right now  and so that was a fun project that we  did with science writing okay so just to  wrap up I want to come back to this  original question that I started with  how does the design use and occupancy of  buildings influence the indoor  microbiome is where I started and so all  of the research that I've shown you has  has looked at the ways in which we  design and use buildings influences the  built environment microbiome and so the  work that we're doing now is trying to  incorporate that top of that triangle  and bringing human health and the human  microbiome back into the picture and so  the what I'm really interested in in  doing with my research is kind of  completing this loop so we've shown in a  lot of ways that humans directly impact  the composition of the indoor microbiome  both by shedding their microbes indoors  and also by the ways that we design  buildings and interact with surfaces in  those buildings all of that can alter  the composition of the indoor microbiome  and so what I'm really intrigued by is  how does what's already in that indoor  environment contribute back to the human  microbiome and ultimately impact human  health and so this is kind of a theme of  our ongoing research projects now I'm  just going to highlight very briefly a  couple of these just to wrap up and so  these are projects that were actively  working on hopefully stay tuned in the  next few months we'll have some results  from each of these and so one of the the  studies that I'm currently doing this is  a follow-up to that microbial cloud  study that I just showed you a few  slides back and in this case instead of  just having one person in a room we have  multiple people sit in a room  cc3 chairs here set up and the questions  that we're asking here are first of all  how far into space does a person's  microbial cloud extend so if someone is  sitting over here can you still pick up  their microbes all the way across the  room or are they staying fairly well  localized and secondly when we do have  multiple people in a room are we still  able to pick out microbes that  distinguish each of these individuals  from each other in the air that were  sampling throughout the room and so I  think that this research could have  really interesting applications for just  understanding the dynamics of  transmission of microbes from person to  person through air and also there's  potential for forensic applications  where you know we can distinguish you  know who was in this room just based on  the air that we sampled in the room at a  given point in time that could be  potentially useful for forensic  applications as well and so this work is  actively ongoing stay tuned for results  another area that we're focusing on  right now is looking at a different  built environment the home microbiome  and so we're specifically asking how  things that interventions that humans  make to their homes by making  weatherization improvements to improve  energy consumption or you know keep your  homes warmer during the winter how does  that impact the composition of the  indoor microbiome and so this is a study  that we're collaborating with members of  the Oregon Research Institute and also a  company called inhabit up in Portland  that puts contractors in touch with  homeowners to do these weatherization  improvements and so we're conducting a  study in two different climatic zones in  Oregon cortland and Bend which is a high  desert region and we're sampling air  inside homes using both petri dishes so  these are just collecting settled dust  and also we're taking a bunch of indoor  air quality measurements for different  chemicals that are circulating through  the air and we're asking how the act of  effectively sealing up the building  envelope of your house by performing  weatherization improvements how is that  shaping the indoor microbiome  composition and so and finally there's a  third study that I'm working on it I'm  really excited about and this is again  looking at how  microbes are potentially being  transmitted to humans via the built  environment and so this is work that I'm  doing with a graduate student in the lab  Ashley Bateman and we're comparing three  different approaches of how microbes  could be getting transferred to humans  and you'll see here that we're working  with pet dogs and houseplants as our  sources of microbes and the reason that  we're focusing on these is because other  research studies have demonstrated that  these organisms contribute a lot of  microbes to the indoor environment and  that could be important for for the  composition of our own human microbiome  and also health and allergies and that  sort of thing and so we're comparing  three different modes of transfer where  one of them is direct contact so can a  person pick up microbes just by touching  the dog or the houseplant the second one  here we're collecting dust from dogs and  plants onto surfaces and then having  people interact with that and so this is  asking the question can we actually  colonize just by touching surfaces in  the built environment and then finally  we're looking at transfer of bio  aerosols and so if you're just in the  same room as a dog or a plant or a room  that was previously occupied by a dog or  a plant does that alone Colin enable  colonization of the human skin  microbiome and so this is work that  we're actively doing and I think it's  really exciting because this is going to  help to close that loop to try to  understand how the built environment  microbiome is potentially shaping the  human microbiome and here's just an  illustration of how we collect some of  these samples and so we have and this  instance we got some house plants in  here where we're collecting dust samples  and then we transfer either through  direct contact or through contact with  surfaces once the skin microbiome and  then we swab over time so this is just a  fun little picture showing how we do  some of that work okay so coming back to  this theme that I started with in the  beginning again we're really trying to  bridge connections between each of these  areas to try to understand how microbial  ecology of the built environment  ultimately impacts human health and so I  just want to close with some open  questions that we're currently thinking  about in our research and one of them  this relates to some  those distance decay patterns that I  showed you earlier where we saw that the  architectural layout of buildings  relates to similarity in microbial  communities and so this leads us to  wonder are there general laws and  building ecology that we can predict and  model based on architectural variables  because we know that in much of ecology  we're interested in seeking general  patterns and principles across space and  time so can we apply similar approaches  to the building ecosystem another big  open question of course is specifically  how the built environment and human  health are related and so something that  I think is really exciting is that you  know for most of the history of  microbiology and built environment  research it's mainly been focused on  pathogens and trying to keep microbes  out of buildings but now that we're  starting to understand that you know we  can't first of all we can't get rid of  those microbes but also that we need a  lot of those microbes to be healthy how  can we actually design the built  environment to promote human health and  so this is a really big motivating  question for us and then finally we're  interested in understanding the  mechanisms of microbial exchange between  buildings air and people and so this  relates to the microbial cloud work that  I just showed you where we're trying to  understand exactly how microbes are  making their way both to and from humans  and the built environment and so with  that we've got a few minutes left I'm  just want to wrap up and acknowledge a  lot of people who have contributed to  this work over the years in the top here  we have the principal investigators so  jessica greene kevin banda y melon  birdie charlie brown all at the  university of oregon we also collaborate  heavily with Curtis Hutton power and  Ralph Halden and then a whole 